TransientOperatorSteadyOperator <p>This operator allows solving transient problems.</p> <p><code>TransientOperator</code> is a template class instantiated with two template parameters: first, the kind of finite element and second, the spatial dimension.</p> <p>Alias declaration for <code>TransientOperator</code> class template</p> <p>The alias <code>TransientOPE</code> is provided by <code>SLOTH</code> namespaces (see the Aliases page) for operator used in unsteady problems</p> <p>The <code>TransientOPE</code> operator must be defined by:</p> <ul> <li>a vector of spatial discretisation objects (see Meshing) [required], </li> <li>a vector of strings specifying the integrators used to model spatial differential operators [required],</li> <li>a set of parameters (see Parameters) [optional],</li> <li>a ODE solver for time stepping [required],</li> <li>a string specifying the integrator used to model time derivative operator [required].</li> </ul> <p>On the size of the vector of spatial discretisation objects</p> <p>In <code>SLOTH</code>, each operator is designed to solve a system of PDEs with a monolithic algorithm. The size of the vector of spatial discretisation objects must be equal to the number of unknowns.</p> <p>Regarding the time stepping, six ODE solvers are available in <code>SLOTH</code>: five are provided by <code>MFEM</code>, and a variable-step BDF2 method is implemented in <code>SLOTH</code>:</p> <ul> <li>implicit methods (solved with the nonlinear algorithm):<ul> <li>The backward Euler method  (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::EulerImplicit</code>),</li> <li>The two-stage, third-order singly diagonal implicit Runge-Kutta method  (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::SDIRK23</code>),</li> <li>The three-stage, third-order singly diagonal implicit Runge-Kutta method  (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::SDIRK33</code>),</li> <li>The variable-step, second-order backward differentiation formula (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::BDF2</code>), see How to use the BDF2 time scheme?,</li> </ul> </li> <li>explicit methods (only the mass matrix is inverted):<ul> <li>The forward Euler method  (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::EulerExplicit</code>),</li> <li>The Runge-Kutta 4 method (<code>SLOTH</code> object <code>TimeScheme::RungeKutta4</code>).</li> </ul> </li> </ul> Time scheme Order Nonlinear solves per time step <code>TimeScheme::EulerImplicit</code> 1 1 <code>TimeScheme::BDF2</code> 2 1 <code>TimeScheme::SDIRK23</code> 3 2 <code>TimeScheme::SDIRK33</code> 3 3 <code>TimeScheme::EulerExplicit</code> 1 - <code>TimeScheme::RungeKutta4</code> 4 - Table 2 - time schemes available in <code>SLOTH</code> <p>Extension of the list of ODE solver in <code>SLOTH</code></p> <p><code>MFEM</code> provides several other ODE solvers. <code>SLOTH</code> development team integrates the most common methods, namely Euler, SDIRK and Runge-Kutta 4 methods, and a variable-step BDF2 method. </p> <p>In the future, this list could be extended to account for other high order methods.</p> <p>Definition of a transient operator</p> <p>This example assume a Cahn-Hilliard problem with two unknowns (<code>phi</code> and <code>mu</code>).  The <code>OPERATOR</code> object, denoted by <code>phasefield_ope</code>, is well declared with a vector of two spatial discretization objects (see the alias <code>SPAS</code> provided by a <code>SLOTH</code> namespace), the <code>CahnHilliard</code> and <code>SplitTimeDerivative</code> integrators and an Euler Implicit time-stepping method.</p> <pre><code>SPAS spatials{&amp;spatial, &amp;spatial};
TransientOPE phasefield_ope(spatials, {"CahnHilliard"}, TimeScheme::EulerImplicit, "SplitTimeDerivative");
</code></pre> <p>Definition of a transient operator with a combination of integrators</p> <p>The integrators for the differential operators can be combined. </p> <p><pre><code>SPAS spatials{&amp;spatial};
TransientOPE phasefield_ope(spatials, {"AllenCahn", "MeltingConstant"}, TimeScheme::EulerImplicit, "TimeDerivative");
</code></pre> In the following example, the operator enables to solve the following equation:</p> <p><p>∂φ∂t=M(∇⋅[λ∇φ]−∂F∂φ)+Mαp′(φ)  \begin{align} \dfrac{\partial \varphi}{\partial t} = M\left(\nabla \cdot \left[\lambda \nabla \varphi\right] - \displaystyle\frac{\partial F}{\partial \varphi}\right)+ M \alpha p'(\varphi)  \end{align} ∂t∂φ​=M(∇⋅[λ∇φ]−∂φ∂F​)+Mαp′(φ)​​</p> where α\alphaα is a constant enthalpy of melting.</p> <p>This operator allows solving steady problems.</p> <p><code>SteadyOperator</code> is a template class instantiated with two template parameters: first, the kind of finite element and second, the spatial dimension.</p> <p>Alias declaration for <code>SteadyOperator</code> class template</p> <p>The alias <code>SteadyOPE</code> is provided by <code>SLOTH</code> namespaces (see the Aliases page) for operator used in steady problems</p> <p>The <code>SteadyOPE</code> operator must be defined by:</p> <ul> <li>a vector of spatial discretisation objects (see Meshing) [required], </li> <li>a vector of strings specifying the integrators used to model spatial differential operators [required],</li> <li>a set of parameters (see Parameters) [optional].</li> </ul> <p>On the size of the vector of spatial discretisation objects</p> <p>In <code>SLOTH</code>, each operator is designed to solve a system of PDEs with a monolithic algorithm. The size of the vector of spatial discretisation objects must be equal to the number of unknowns.</p> <p>Definition of a steady operator</p> <p>This example assume an Allen-Cahn problem with one unknowns (<code>phi</code>).  The <code>OPERATOR</code> object, denoted by <code>phasefield_ope</code>, is well declared with a vector of one spatial discretization object and the <code>AllenCahn</code>  integrator.</p> <pre><code>SPAS spatials{&amp;spatial, &amp;spatial};
SteadyOPE phasefield_ope(spatials, {"AllenCahn"});
</code></pre>

A constantA SLOTH built-inYour own formula <pre><code>Coefficient capillary(Glossary::Capillary, lambda);
</code></pre> <p>No scheme; the derivatives are zero.</p> <p><code>W(omega)</code>, <code>Fw(omega)</code>, <code>H()</code>, <code>Log(a)</code>, <code>GradientEnergy(lambda)</code>: double wells, interpolation function, logarithmic energy and gradient energy, available in any input file (see the common list of coefficients). They are themselves generated by <code>Sympy</code> from a JSON file of the <code>SLOTH</code> kernel, exactly as your own coefficients will be.</p> <pre><code>{
    "expression": "…", 
    "variables": "c", 
    "class_name": "Gc", 
    "outputfile": "MyCoefficients"
}
</code></pre> <p><code>Sympy</code> writes the class <code>Gc</code> and its derivatives; it is then used as <code>Coefficient(…, Gc())</code>.</p>

Two different problemsN identical problems <pre><code>auto cc = Coupling("CH/AC", ac_pb, ch_pb);
</code></pre> <pre><code>std::vector&lt;TransientPB&gt; ac_pbs;
// … fill the N problems …
auto cc = setCoupling&lt;N&gt;("Multigrains", ac_pbs);
</code></pre> <p><code>N</code> must equal the size of the vector.</p> <p>A <code>Coupling</code> has a name and a list of problems, solved one after another at each time step, in the order they are written: this is a partitioned scheme. Each problem reads the fields of the others through its auxiliary variables. Inside a problem, on the other hand, the unknowns are solved together: <code>φ</code> and <code>μ</code> of Cahn-Hilliard form a monolithic system.</p> <p>See the Couplings page of the User Manual.</p>
